    "reasoning": "The Firestore data structure is designed to support a modular, role-based super-app, Zipp. Key considerations include: \n\n1.  **Authorization Independence:** Denormalization is used extensively to avoid `get()` calls in security rules. For example, the `users` collection stores the user's `role`, eliminating the need to fetch user data when validating operations on other collections.  This makes authorization rules simpler, robust, and easily debuggable. The `vendorId` is included in related entities like `promotions`, `orders`, `bookings`, and `redemptions` to avoid needing to fetch the parent vendor document for authorization.\n2.  **Structural Segregation:** Data with different access requirements is separated into distinct collections. User-generated content (listings) and vendor-managed content (promotions, offerings) are stored in separate collections, each with appropriate security rules. Offerings and Reviews are now a sub-collection of vendors.\n3.  **Access Modeling:** User-owned data (listings, orders, bookings) are stored under the `/users/{userId}` path, enabling path-based authorization. Vendor-owned data is stored in a subcollection under the vendor document, which allows for strong ownership rules.\n4.  **Performance Optimization:** Singleton documents like `featuredMerchants` and `todaysHotDeals` cache curated data, populated by scheduled Cloud Functions. This avoids costly, scalable queries on every homepage load, thus reducing read operations and preventing quota exhaustion. With the removal of the root /offerings collection, search performance is now tied directly to Firestore's indexing capabilities on the /vendors collection, simplifying the architecture.\n\nSpecific denormalization strategies:\n\n*   User `role` is stored directly in the `users` collection, so rules can validate user roles without additional reads.\n*   `vendorId` is included on all vendor-related documents to allow validation of vendor ownership without reading the vendor document.\n* A `geocodeCache` collection has been added to cache results from the Nominatim API, reducing API calls and improving performance. The document ID is the normalized search query.\n* A `userActivityLogs` collection has been added to record user actions. It is write-only for users to ensure data integrity.\n* An `analytics` collection has been added to store monthly aggregated usage data. It is read-only for admins."
